THIS PATCH IS UNBUILT AND UNTESTED. IT HAS NEVER BEEN COMPILED, RUN IN A GAME,
OR EXERCISED BY ANY NATIVE TEST. DO NOT SHIP OR SIGN A LIBRARY BUILT FROM IT
UNTIL THE MEASUREMENTS BELOW HAVE BEEN COLLECTED AND REVIEWED.

Background F10 reload (design and best-effort patch)
=====================================================

Status
  * Separate, optional design patch. tools/PreparedLoading/native.patch is
    unchanged and remains the qualified source patch; nothing here is part of
    the candidate described in COMPLETION-STATUS.md.
  * Applies ON TOP OF the tree produced by native.patch (pinned base
    3DMigoto/XXMI-Libs a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd plus
    native.patch). It is not a patch against the upstream base.
  * Every hunk, including DirectX11/IniHandler.cpp, is generated with git diff
    against the full pinned upstream tree with native.patch applied, and
    `git apply --check` passes on that tree.
  * Upstream identifiers used here were checked against that tree:
    RegisterKeyBinding(iniKey, keyName, shared_ptr<InputListener>, auto_repeat,
    down_delay, up_delay, scope, configuration_owned) with InputCallbacks,
    G->gWipeUserConfig, CustomResource::resource/view, and the CustomShader
    stage/state members (from native.patch's ATTACH_SHADER). Nothing has been
    compiled, so a build may still need small fixes.
  * Expected usage after a build:
        git apply --check background-reload.patch   (on the post-native tree)
        git apply background-reload.patch
    git apply ignores this text before the first per-file header.

Configuration ([ModLoading] in the importer's d3dx.ini)
  background_reload = 0      default; F10 is the unchanged legacy reload
  background_reload = 1      F10 becomes a background reload (see a)
  legacy_reload_key =        optional; default ctrl no_alt no_shift VK_F10
  background_reload requires mode != off, transactional_activation = 1 and
  general_fast_refresh = 1. The launcher writes background_reload only when
  the signed capability metadata lists the "background_reload" feature, which
  tools/preparation-capabilities.py adds for a build of this patch.

Design
  (a) F10 as a background reload.
      FlagConfigReload notes an explicit key press (NoteReloadKeyPressed).
      ReloadConfig first calls ModLoading::TryBeginBackgroundReload. It
      accepts only a key press, never the delayed initial load, the
      wipe-user-config variant (G->gWipeUserConfig), a SINGLETHREADED device,
      a disabled/admission-blocked general pipeline, or a pending forced
      legacy request. When accepted, ReloadConfig returns without touching the
      live generation. At the next Present, PumpRuntime (which already runs
      before frame commands take the configuration pin in RunFrameActions)
      calls PumpBackgroundReload. It either claims the already valid pending
      general candidate or schedules a forced full build through the existing
      ScheduleGeneralBuild / GeneralBuildWorker (detached production
      parser/linker, dependency closure, watches). PumpGeneralConfiguration
      treats the claimed candidate as activation-requested, so it publishes
      through the existing StageConfigurationRuntime /
      PublishStagedConfiguration path at the frame boundary as soon as the
      complete-graph proof holds. The old generation stays live until then.
      Fallback to the legacy synchronous reload on the next frame (overlay
      notice) when: the candidate becomes invalid (any compatibility reason
      from the general pipeline, e.g. arbitrary initializers, pools, mutable
      resources, shader-regex changes), bounded migration fails, GPU
      preparation is unavailable, or no candidate publishes within 10 s.
      A separate key, legacy_reload_key (default Ctrl+F10), always forces the
      legacy reload/reset, as does the wipe-user-config binding.
      Semantic difference: a background publication carries the latest
      persistent variables and recomputes bounded pure initializers only. It
      does not re-run arbitrary [Constants] initialization, re-parse process
      settings ([System], [Rendering], [Hunting] ...), or reset overrides the
      way the legacy route does. Those cases must use the legacy key.
  (b) GPU resource creation on worker threads.
      For a claimed candidate, PreparationPlanner::PumpWorker replaces the
      per-frame Pump when the device is not D3D11_CREATE_DEVICE_SINGLETHREADED
      and D3D11_FEATURE_THREADING reports DriverConcurrentCreates. The owner
      thread keeps all admission and bookkeeping (process budget, cache room,
      per-frame upload_budget_mb cap, failure keys, cache insertion, alias
      keys); one detached worker per planner only calls the shared Create()
      code (CreateDetached) on the original, unhooked ID3D11Device that
      PumpRuntime already receives (GetPassThroughOrigDevice1), always with
      initial data and never with a device context. Each job holds an AddRef on
      the device; retirement cancels without joining, so the owner never waits
      for the worker. Each worker creation holds LockResourceCreationMode for
      that one driver call. Without concurrent-create support the existing
      bounded per-frame Pump runs (create_budget_ms / upload_budget_mb), with
      the F10 press acting as an explicit loading window for that candidate
      only. preparation_loading_window keeps its meaning for everything else.
      SINGLETHREADED devices keep the legacy F10 (native.patch already refuses
      complete-graph GPU preparation for them).
  (c) Paced release of old generations.
      CompleteGraphRetirement already frees a retired graph only after its GPU
      event query completes and no reader pins it. With background_reload on,
      that graph goes to one of four fixed slots; PumpRuntime then releases at
      most 16 CustomResource/CustomShader entries' COM children per frame on
      the owner thread before the remaining CPU-only teardown takes the
      existing Disposal() path. A full table, or a recreated device, keeps the
      original one-shot release.
      Slots never outlive their owner: a device mismatch is detected before
      the owner-thread check (so a replacement owner/device still reaches the
      cleanup), and RetireRuntimeDevice and a legacy reload drain every slot
      back to the one-shot release. The drain never blocks (a COM Release
      inside the pump can reenter device retirement); if the slot mutex is
      busy, the next pump takes every slot first. Slots are not drained at
      process exit. A mock-type C++ harness of these functions (not D3D)
      showed 4 of 4 graphs pinned after 100 replacement-owner pumps before
      this, and none pinned with all four slots reusable after.

Risks
  * Worker creation and the render thread contend for the driver and for the
    creation-mode lock; a single large texture creation can still block the
    render thread for its duration. Drivers that report concurrent creates may
    still serialize internally.
  * The original device can outlive its HackerDevice wrapper until an in-flight
    worker job drops its reference; the final Release may then run on the
    worker thread.
  * Cache insertion happens after MakeRoom on a later frame, so the cache can
    briefly exceed its cap and an insert can fail (counted as a failure).
  * Peak VRAM is old + new generation until the swap and paced release
    complete. The general GPU budget still bounds the candidate, not driver
    overhead or residency.
  * Behavioral difference from legacy F10 (see a). Users must know the legacy
    key; an overlay notice is shown on request and on every fallback.
  * Releasing CustomResource/CustomShader children early assumes their
    destructors tolerate null members (upstream checks for null).

Must be measured in game before any release (per game, same scene, paired
legacy vs background, at least 20 F10 presses each, several mod sets)
  * PresentMon frame time: p50, p99 and max over the 2 s around each F10 press
    and over the release window afterwards; the goal is p99 within normal
    gameplay variance.
  * Stall counts: frames above 2x and 4x the median frame time per reload,
    plus native LoadTrace Activation/GpuCreation/ViewCreation durations and
    time spent waiting on the creation-mode lock on the render thread.
  * VRAM: peak dedicated GPU memory (PresentMon/GPUView or DXGI
    QueryVideoMemoryInfo) during build, swap and release; compare with the
    general GPU budget.
  * Time from key press to visible change, fallback rate and reasons, and
    first-correct-frame visual comparison against a legacy reload.
  * Device-removed, alt-tab and resolution-change behavior during a pending
    background reload; repeated presses while a build is in flight.

